ou will land in a city of glass buildings, traffic jams, and hip coffee boutiques. The food in restaurants is fantastic and plentiful. * **Prepare for persistent poverty:** Outside the city centers and in the countryside, you will see subsistence farming and real hardship. It won't be a uniform disaster, but it's a poor nation with many poor people. This is not the same as an active famine. * **Prepare for modern-day displacement:** Be aware that Ethiopia is currently dealing with humanitarian crises from the recent Tigray War and drought in the south, leading to displaced populations. This is not all of Ethiopia, but it is a present reality. The images you might have seen recently from those areas can look eerily reminiscent of the 80s, which is tragically triggering. * **Prepare for dignity and generosity:** The most shocking thing you'll encounter, given the stereotype, is the deep, profound culture of hospitality and pride. You will be invited for coffee, welcomed warmly, and encounter people who are rightfully proud of their history as the only uncolonized African nation, their ancient script, and their culinary traditions. So, the Live Aid images aren't "made up," but they are a 40-year-old single frame of a specific catastrophe in a vast, ancient, and rapidly changing nation. Go with the knowledge that the frame was real, but the movie was always much longer, more complex, and is still being written. You are preparing to see the whole movie.
