arts of Amhara, Oromia, and the Southern region. FEWS NET classifies many of these as "Stressed." This means millions of poor subsistence farming families are living on the edge. A shock—a lost harvest, sickness—can push them into crisis. This is where the massive, ongoing, long-term aid programs (food-for-work, resilience building) operate. This is real suffering, but it's a different reality from an active famine camp. Markets function, children go to school, and daily life grinds on with immense hardship, but not mass starvation. - **Stable Metropolitan and Non-Crisis Zones:** Addis Ababa, Bahir Dar, Gondar, Hawassa, and the rural tourist areas around Lalibela are not listed on these aid maps. They are not aid priorities because people are not in need of emergency food assistance there. The millions of people living in these cities are dealing with inflation, yes, but they are not in a humanitarian crisis. The aid workers aren't there not because they’re ignoring suffering, but because by their own strict metrics, there isn't a food crisis to respond to. So, when you say you trust the aid workers, you are trusting them. And they are telling you, through their own crisis mapping, that the country is a mosaic. The "Ethiopia" of their urgent appeal is a very specific set of regions, not the café where you'll have a macchiato. ### 2. Why This Doesn't Feel Like a Sales Pitch (I Hope) A tourism board wants you to see only the historic churches and stunning mountains and pretend the rest doesn't exist. That's a lie of omission. An honest reckoning, which I think you're demanding, looks like this: You are entering a country that is simultaneously: - A humanitarian crisis zone (in regions clearly marked as no-go for tourists). - A place of chronic, grinding rural poverty (which you will see from the window of a car). - A place of proud, functional, vibrant normality (which you will experience in the cities, restaurants, and historical sites). You will not have to prepare 