estation in the areas you'll visit. If your conscience is troubled, that's a far more meaningful way to respond than just feeling bad. It turns you from a passive observer into a small, respectful part of the solution. In the end, the aid workers' map and the travel blogger's map are describing the same country, but through the lens of different regions. You don't need to reject the aid worker to travel. You need to use their map to know exactly where the crisis is, and understand that you are traveling in the other, equally real, parts of the picture. Does that help bridge the gap between the alarming statistics you trust and the trip you're considering?
